['Here's a rough start on how you might implement such a function in Java using the Spring framework for handling HTTP requests and caching. Note that this is just a sketch of an idea and does not cover all the details mentioned above. In practice, you would need to handle a lot more scenarios and edge cases:

```java
import org.springframework.cache.annotation.Cacheable;
import org.springframework.http.*;
import org.springframework.web.client.RestTemplate;
import java.util.concurrent.CompletableFuture;
import java.util.logging.Logger;

public class PokemonService {
    private static final Logger logger = Logger.getLogger(PokemonService.class.getName());

    @Cacheable("pokemons")
    public String getWeaknesses(int pokemonId) throws InterruptedException {
        try {
            // Assume Pokemon class has id and type fields
            CompletableFuture<Pokemon> pokemonPromise = getPokemonDetailsAsync(pokemonId);

            Pokemon pokemon = pokemonPromise.join();

            if (pokemon == null) {
                logger.severe("No such Pokemon exists.");
                return "Unable to find weaknesses for the given Pokemon.";
            } else {
                switch(pokemon.getType()) {
                    case "fire":
                        return "Water";
                    // Add more cases based on different Pokemon types...
                    default:
                        logger.severe("Unknown type of Pokemon provided.");
                        return "Unable to determine weaknesses for the given Pokemon.";
                }
            }
        } catch (Exception e) {
            logger.severe(e.getMessage());
            Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
        }

        return null;
    }

    @Async("threadPoolTaskExecutor")
    public CompletableFuture<Pokemon> getPokemonDetailsAsync(int pokemonId) throws InterruptedException {
        RestTemplate restTemplate = new RestTemplate();

        HttpHeaders headers = new HttpHeaders();
        headers.setContentType(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON);
        HttpEntity<String> entity = new HttpEntity<>("parameters", headers);

        CompletableFuture<Pokemon> pokemonPromise = restTemplate.exchange(
            "https://api.pokemontypes.com/types/" + pokemonId, 
            HttpMethod.GET, entity, Pokemon.class)
            .getBody();

        return pokemonPromise;
    }
}
```
This code assumes that you have a properly configured thread pool executor bean named "threadPoolTaskExecutor", which is responsible for asynchronous execution of tasks. You also need to enable Spring's cache and async capabilities, which are disabled by default. Note that this solution doesn't handle rate limits and the handling of unexpected values or null responses from external data sources.
']

['Here is a basic implementation of the described functionality in C. It's important to note that this example does not include all aspects you specified (like configuring default parameters through environment variables, logging, etc.), but it should give you a good starting point for dynamic library loading and caching. 

Please compile with -ldl flag as `dlopen` is in `libdl`.
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <dlfcn.h>

// Define a struct to manage the cryptographic libraries
typedef struct CryptoLib {
    void* handle;
    int (*init)(void); // Function pointers for hash and cipher functions
    char* (*hash)(const char *, size_t);
    char* (*encrypt)(const char *, const char *, size_t);
} CryptoLib;

// Create a cache to store the availability status of each library
CryptoLib* libCache = NULL; // Use a hashtable or similar for more efficient access and update
int cacheSize = 0;

void* loadLibrary(const char* libName) {
    void* handle = dlopen(libName, RTLD_LAZY);

    if (!handle) {
        // Handle error here - logging can be custom implemented
        fprintf(stderr, "Failed to open library: %s\n", dlerror());
        return NULL;
    }

    // Dynamically allocate memory for the struct and cache it
    libCache = realloc(libCache, (cacheSize + 1) * sizeof(CryptoLib));
    if (!libCache) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Failed to allocate memory\n");
        return NULL;
    }

    // Initialize the struct and store it in the cache
    CryptoLib lib = {.handle = handle};
    libCache[cacheSize] = lib;
    cacheSize++;

    return handle;
}

int isLibraryAvailable(const char* libName) {
    for (int i = 0; i < cacheSize; ++i) {
        if (!strcmp(libCache[i].handle, libName)) {
            return 1; // Library found in cache
        }
    }

    // If library not found in cache, attempt to load it
    void* handle = loadLibrary(libName);
    if (handle) {
        // Update the cache with the new status
        for (int i = 0; i < cacheSize; ++i) {
            if (!strcmp(libCache[i].handle, libName)) {
                libCache[i].isAvailable = 1;
                return 1; // Library found and loaded successfully
            }
        }
    }

    return 0; // Failed to load library
}
```
Remember that this is a basic implementation. In real-world applications, you would need to add error checking for `dlsym` as well, handle memory management more carefully (for example, using reference counting), and implement other features like logging and environment variable configuration as requested. 

Also note that there are more efficient ways to achieve dynamic library loading, such as using the Address Space Layout Randomization (ASLR) feature of the operating system, which can lead to consistent addresses for libraries across multiple runs of a program. However, these techniques require careful handling and could complicate the code.
']

["This problem is complex because it requires a deep understanding of C programming language, OpenSSL library, thread synchronization concepts, and performance optimization techniques. Here's an example solution based on the description above:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <openssl/evp.h>
#include <openssl/err.h>
#include <pthread.h>

// Define a global lock for thread safety
static pthread_mutex_t *lock;

char* processString(char *input, int mode) {
    EVP_CIPHER_CTX *ctx = NULL;  // Context for encryption and decryption
    char *output = NULL;  // Output buffer

    // Acquire the lock before entering critical section
    pthread_mutex_lock(lock);

    do {
        ctx = EVP_CIPHER_CTX_new();
        if (ctx == NULL) {
            ERR_print_errors_fp(stderr);
            break;
        }

        // Initialize encryption or decryption operation based on mode
        if (!EVP_CipherInit_ex(ctx, EVP_aes_256_cbc(), NULL, key, iv, mode)) {
            ERR_print_errors_fp(stderr);
            break;
        }

        // Allocate memory for output buffer
        int len = strlen(input), outl = 0;
        if (!EVP_CipherUpdate(ctx, (unsigned char*)output, &outl, (const unsigned char*)input, len)) {
            ERR_print_errors_fp(stderr);
            break;
        }

        // Finalize encryption or decryption operation
        if (!EVP_CipherFinal_ex(ctx, (unsigned char*)output+outl, &len)) {
            ERR_print_errors_fp(stderr);
            break;
        }

    } while (0);  // Breaks and continues are used to handle errors correctly

    if (ctx != NULL) EVP_CIPHER_CTX_free(ctx); // Free context if allocated

    // Release the lock after leaving critical section
    pthread_mutex_unlock(lock);

    return output;
}
```
In this code:
- The `processString` function is responsible for encryption and decryption using OpenSSL library.
- It supports encrypting and decrypting based on the mode provided.
- AES with a 256 bit key size is used as an example, but any cipher can be selected in the call to EVP_CipherInit_ex().
- Errors during encryption or decryption are handled by printing error messages.
- The function is thread safe because it uses a global lock before accessing shared resources and releases the lock after exiting critical section of code. 

This solution does not cover command line options parsing, file reading, and memory optimization. Adding such features would further complicate this code and may require additional libraries or techniques for performance optimizations. This is just a simple example to illustrate the concept. For real-world application, it's recommended to use more complete solutions or existing libraries designed specifically for handling encryption/decryption needs in C with OpenSSL library.
"]

['Here is a simplified example of how you might accomplish this in C using the POSIX thread library for semaphores. In a real-world scenario, you would likely need additional features such as support for other platforms (Windows), logging facilities, error handling etc. 

Please note that actual implementation may vary based on your requirements and constraints. Here is an example of how one could approach it:

```c
#include <pthread.h>
#include <semaphore.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <errno.h>

void manage_sem(int init_value, int increment) {
    sem_t* sem;

    if ((sem = malloc(sizeof(sem_t))) == NULL) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Failed to allocate memory for semaphore\n");
        return;
    }

    // Initialize the semaphore with dynamic value.
    if (sem_init(sem, 0, init_value)) {
        perror("Failed to initialize semaphore");
        free(sem);
        return;
    }

    // Perform an increment operation on semaphore in a safe way by handling potential errors.
    if (sem_post(sem, increment) == -1 && errno != EOVERFLOW) {
        perror("Failed to increment semaphore");
        goto cleanup;
    } else if (errno == EOVERFLOW) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Semaphore overflow\n");
        goto cleanup;
    }

    // Perform some operations...

cleanup:
    if (sem_destroy(sem)) {
        perror("Failed to destroy semaphore");
    } else {
        free(sem);
    }
}
```

This is a simplified example and doesn't consider thread safety or concurrent calls. For thread-safety, you would typically use locks (`pthread_mutex_t`) around semaphore operations to ensure they are atomic. This implementation also doesn't take into account system load or available memory for resource allocation optimization as it can greatly affect the efficiency and robustness of your program. However, these aspects were not specified in your original request.
']

