["Here is a sample solution for your task using C++14 and STL:

```cpp
#include <algorithm>
#include <mutex>
#include <vector>
#include <string>

std::mutex vecMutex;  // mutex to handle multithreading

void insertAfterFirstOccurrence(std::vector<std::string>& vec, const std::string& target, const std::string& insert) {
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(vecMutex);  // lock mutex to ensure thread safety

    auto it = std::find(vec.begin(), vec.end(), target);
    if (it != vec.end())
        vec.insert(++it, insert);
    else
        vec.push_back(insert);
}
```
In this solution, we use the STL algorithm function `std::find` to locate the first occurrence of `target` in the vector. If an occurrence is found, we insert `insert` after it using the vector's `insert` method. If not, `insert` is appended to the end of the vector with the `push_back` method. 

To handle multiple threads concurrently modifying the same vector, we use a mutex (`vecMutex`) around the critical section where the vector is modified. This ensures that only one thread can modify the vector at a time, thus preventing data corruption or performance bottlenecks in a multi-threaded environment.

To handle real-time updates from an external source, we would need to integrate this function into a larger system and define how/when these updates happen. This could be done with callback functions, message queues, or other forms of interprocess communication depending on the specifics of your application. 

