["Here is a JavaScript implementation that meets all the requirements:

```js
function concatenateChunks(chunkCount, chunkSize, buffers) {
    let result = Buffer.alloc(0);
    let totalBytes = 0;

    for (let i = 0; i < buffers.length && totalBytes < chunkCount * chunkSize; i++) {
        const buffer = buffers[i];

        if (!Buffer.isBuffer(buffer)) continue; // ensure buffer is valid

        let byteOffset = 0;
        while (byteOffset < buffer.length && totalBytes < chunkCount * chunkSize) {
            const bytesLeftInChunk = chunkCount * chunkSize - totalBytes;
            const sliceEnd = Math.min(buffer.length, byteOffset + bytesLeftInChunk);

            result = Buffer.concat([result, buffer.slice(byteOffset, sliceEnd)]);
            totalBytes += sliceEnd - byteOffset;
            byteOffset = sliceEnd;
        }
    }

    return result;
}
```

This function starts by creating a new empty `Buffer` and initializing variables to keep track of the current total bytes processed. 

Then, it iterates over each input buffer. For each buffer, it loops until either all requested chunks have been read or all data has been processed from this buffer. Within this inner loop, it calculates how many bytes are left in the current chunk and slices off a portion of the buffer up to that limit, adding this slice to the result buffer.

The function continues processing with the next buffers as long as the total number of processed bytes is less than the requested number of chunks multiplied by the size of each chunk. 

It also skips any non-buffer inputs in the input array and ensures that it doesn't try to read more bytes from a buffer than it actually has, thus preventing out-of-bounds exceptions.
"]

