["Below are code snippets that implement the requested functionality using OpenSSL for AES encryption. Due to the complexity and nature of this task, the solution involves several steps like initializing context, setting up password based key derivation, encrypting/decrypting data and finally cleanup. 

```c
#include <openssl/aes.h>
#include <openssl/evp.h>
#include <openssl/sha.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <stdio.h>

typedef struct {
    EVP_CIPHER_CTX* ctx;
} AES256Context;

AES256Context* initializeCipher(const char* password) {
    AES256Context* aes = malloc(sizeof(AES256Context));
    unsigned char key[SHA_DIGEST_LENGTH]; // or 32 bytes for SHA-256
    unsigned char iv[AES_BLOCK_SIZE]; // 16 bytes

    memset(key, 0, sizeof(key));
    memset(iv, 'z', sizeof(iv)); // zero padding

    EVP_BytesToKey(EVP_aes_256_cbc(), EVP_sha1(), NULL, (unsigned char*)password, strlen(password), 1, key, iv);

    aes->ctx = EVP_CIPHER_CTX_new();
    EVP_EncryptInit_ex(aes->ctx, EVP_aes_256_cbc(), NULL, key, iv);

    return aes;
}

void cleanupCipher(AES256Context* aes) {
    EVP_CIPHER_CTX_free(aes->ctx);
    free(aes);
}
``` 

The `initializeCipher` function initializes the AES-256-CBC cipher and sets up password based key derivation using SHA-1. The EVP_BytesToKey function fills in the key and IV buffers for use by OpenSSL.

The `cleanupCipher` function disables and cleans up the cipher context when it is no longer needed. 

This solution assumes that cleanup and disabling of the cipher are handled properly, and potential concurrency issues are managed outside of this function.

