['Here is a sample implementation using C# Tasks for parallel processing and ConcurrentDictionary for thread-safe dictionary operations:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Concurrent;
using System.IO;
using System.Security.Cryptography;
using System.Threading;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

public class FileHasher
{
    private ConcurrentDictionary<string, string> fileHashDict = new ConcurrentDictionary<string, string>();
    private CancellationTokenSource cancellationTokenSource = new CancellationTokenSource();

    public void ProcessFiles(string[] filePaths)
    {
        try
        {
            Parallel.ForEach(filePaths, new ParallelOptions { MaxDegreeOfParallelism = 4 }, filePath =>
            {
                cancellationTokenSource.Token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested();

                string hash = CalculateSHA256Hash(filePath);
                if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(hash))
                    fileHashDict[filePath] = hash;
            });
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException)
        {
            Console.WriteLine("Operation cancelled.");
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            // Log the exception for debugging purposes
            Console.WriteLine($"An error occurred: {ex}");
        }
    }

    private string CalculateSHA256Hash(string filePath)
    {
        try
        {
            using (var stream = new BufferedStream(File.OpenRead(filePath), 1048576 /* 1 MB buffer size */))
            {
                using (var sha256 = SHA256.Create())
                {
                    byte[] hashBytes = sha256.ComputeHash(stream);
                    return BitConverter.ToString(hashBytes).Replace("-", "").ToLower();
                }
            }
        }
        catch (IOException ex)
        {
            Console.WriteLine($"Failed to read file {filePath}: {ex}");
            return null;
        }
    }

    public void CancelProcessing()
    {
        cancellationTokenSource.Cancel();
    }

    // For testing purposes only, remove in a production environment
    public ConcurrentDictionary<string, string> GetFileHashes()
    {
        return fileHashDict;
    }
}
```
The `ProcessFiles` method takes an array of file paths and calculates their SHA-256 hashes concurrently using multiple threads. It uses a buffered stream to read the files efficiently, with a buffer size set to 1 MB. The SHA-256 hash is calculated by reading blocks of data from the file, reducing memory usage compared to reading the entire file at once.

The `GetFileHashes` method is for testing purposes only and should be removed in production environments. It returns the dictionary mapping each file path to its SHA-256 hash. All exceptions are logged and not rethrown to avoid unhandled exceptions. This makes the code more robust and easier to debug.
']

['Here is an example of a C# function that meets your specifications using AES encryption in .NET's `System.Security.Cryptography` namespace. This implementation assumes you have access to a logging framework (like NLog or Serilog) for error handling and logging, and also requires the key/iv are 16 bytes long:

```csharp
using System;
using System.IO;
using System.Security.Cryptography;
using System.Text;
using NLog; //or any other logging framework you prefer

public class AESDecrypter
{
    private static readonly Logger logger = LogManager.GetCurrentClassLogger();

    public byte[] Decrypt(byte[] ciphertext, byte[] key, byte[] iv)
    {
        try
        {
            using (var aesAlg = new AesCryptoServiceProvider())
            {
                aesAlg.KeySize = 128; // 16 bytes for AES-128
                aesAlg.BlockSize = 128;

                aesAlg.IV = iv;
                aesAlg.Key = key;

                aesAlg.Mode = CipherMode.CBC;
                aesAlg.Padding = PaddingMode.PKCS7;

                var decryptor = aesAlg.CreateDecryptor(aesAlg.Key, aesAlg.IV);

                using (MemoryStream msDecrypt = new MemoryStream())
                {
                    using (CryptoStream csDecrypt = new CryptoStream(msDecrypt, decryptor, CryptoStreamMode.Write))
                    {
                        csDecrypt.Write(ciphertext, 0, ciphertext.Length);
                        csDecrypt.FlushFinalBlock();

                        return msDecrypt.ToArray();
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            logger.Error(ex, "An error occurred during decryption");

            // Depending on your requirements and the nature of errors, re-throw or handle differently
            throw;
        }
    }
}
```

The code above creates a `AESDecrypter` class with a `Decrypt` method that takes in the ciphertext as a byte array along with the key and IV. It uses AesCryptoServiceProvider for decryption which is recommended by Microsoft due to its performance benefits over other .NET cryptographic classes like AesManaged or AesCng.

Error handling is done using try-catch block which catches exceptions and logs them using a logging framework (e.g., NLog in this case). The error message contains details about the exception, useful for debugging and monitoring purposes. 

Please replace `NLog` with your preferred logging framework.
