    assert(*keys != NULL);

    *precisionValues = malloc(numRecords * sizeof(double));
    assert(*precisionValues != NULL);

    *records = malloc(numRecords * sizeof(dataRecord));
    assert(*records != NULL);

    srand((unsigned int) time(NULL)); // Initialize random seed 
    for (size_t i = 0; i < numRecords; ++i) {
        (*offsets)[i] = rand();
        (*keys)[i] = rand() % INT_MAX; // Specify your key range here.
        (*precisionValues)[i] = ((double)rand() / (double)RAND_MAX); // Specify your precision value range here.

        char buffer[20];
        sprintf(buffer, "%zu", i);
        (*records)[i].value = rand();
        (*records)[i].data = ((double)rand() / (double)RAND_MAX); 
        (*records)[i].text = malloc(strlen(buffer) + 1);
        strcpy((*records)[i].text, buffer);
    }

    fclose(file);
}
```
Note:
- The function `cleanup` frees all dynamically allocated memory.
- The function `processData` reads data from a CSV file and stores it in the specified arrays. If an error occurs during file reading, a corresponding message is printed to `stdout`.
- For simplicity, we've implemented rudimentary data type checks (e.g., using `isdigit()` for numbers) but these are not exhaustive nor complete. Properly parsing CSV files can be quite complex and usually involves error checking as well.
- The memory allocation size is calculated in bytes, taking into account the size of individual data type. 
- We use `assert` to check that the memory was successfully allocated before proceeding with further operations. If an out-of-memory condition occurs, the program will terminate.
- Random values are generated using the `rand()` function. You may want to seed it differently for different runs of your application if you need more randomness or reproducibility.
