["Here is an example of how you might implement such a function using pthreads for concurrency control and glib's GList for dynamic resizing and iteration. This code does not include database lookups or error logging, but it gives you a good starting point for a real-world system that needs to handle these complexities:

```c
#include <pthread.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <glib.h>

typedef struct {
    int UEID;
    GList* CCEs;
    bool CRC_flag;
} CCCI_t;

// Mutex to protect access to the list
pthread_mutex_t listMutex = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;
GList* CCCIs = NULL;

void addCCCI(int UEID, GList* CCEs, bool CRC_flag) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&listMutex);

    // Allocate memory for new CCCI and populate it
    CCCI_t* ccci = malloc(sizeof(CCCI_t));
    ccci->UEID = UEID;
    ccci->CCEs = g_list_copy(CCEs); // Deep copy of the list
    ccci->CRC_flag = CRC_flag;

    // Add to the end of the list
    CCCIs = g_list_append(CCCIs, ccci);

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&listMutex);
}

void removeAndFreeCCCI(GList* node) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&listMutex);

    CCCI_t* ccci = (CCCI_t*)node->data;

    // Free memory associated with the CCEs list and the CCCI struct itself
    g_list_free(ccci->CCEs);
    free(ccci);

    // Remove from the list
    CCCIs = g_list_delete_link(CCCIs, node);

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&listMutex);
}
```
Please note that this code assumes you're using Glib and pthreads. For more complex projects, it would likely be better to use a higher-level library or framework like Qt for cross-platform development, or even C++ with STL for better type safety and exception handling. 

