['Here is a simple implementation of the required function in C++. It uses std::mutex and std::ofstream to handle concurrency safety, file writing, and timestamps respectively. However, please note that this is a basic version of the solution and it may not cover all possible edge cases or performance issues depending on your specific use case or environment.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <fstream>
#include <chrono>
#include <thread>
#include <mutex>

std::mutex cityResourcesMutex;  // Mutex for synchronization
int cityResources = 100;       // Global variable representing city resources

void weatherSimulation() {
    static std::time_t lastChange = std::chrono::system_clock::to_time_t(std::chrono::system_clock::now());

    if (std::difftime(std::chrono::system_clock::to_time_t(std::chrono::system_clock::now()), lastChange) < 15 * 60) { // Cooldown period is 15 minutes.
        return;
    }

    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(cityResourcesMutex);  // Lock resources for safe access.

    if (cityResources < 10) {  // If city does not have enough resources, no change performed and function returns immediately.
        return;
    }

    cityResources -= 10;  // Consume resources.

    std::ofstream logFile("weather_log.txt", std::ios_base::app);  // Open file in append mode.

    int weatherCondition = rand() % 3;  // Randomly select a weather condition.

    lastChange = std::chrono::system_clock::to_time_t(std::chrono::system_clock::now());

    switch (weatherCondition) {
        case 0:
            logFile << "Sunny, timestamp: " << lastChange << "\n";
            std::cout << "Weather condition changed to Sunny.\n";
            break;
        case 1:
            logFile << "Rainy, timestamp: " << lastChange << "\n";
            std::cout << "Weather condition changed to Rainy.\n";
            break;
        case 2:
            logFile << "Snowy, timestamp: " << lastChange << "\n";
            std::cout << "Weather condition changed to Snowy.\n";
            break;
    }
}
```
This function can be called from multiple threads concurrently without any race conditions as long as they properly lock the `cityResourcesMutex`. It uses a log file (`weather_log.txt`) for logging all weather changes and their timestamps. However, it may not handle high-frequency calls efficiently due to its use of std::ofstream's append mode which can be slow with large files and many writes. You might need to consider other strategies or APIs suitable for your specific requirements.
