['Here is the code snippet that fulfills the above requirements but please note that this is a simplified version of a web server application handling requests with background processes. It lacks error checking, logging, HTTP parsing and doesn't fully implement all the trade-offs mentioned in your request. Also, it's not recommended to use `waitpid()` like in this example without considering the potential issues. Use libraries for production level code.

```C
#include <stdio.h>
#include <signal.h>
#include <sys/types.h>
#include <sys/wait.h>
#include <unistd.h>

// Define the maximum number of processes to handle.
#define MAX_PROCESSES 50
pid_t pids[MAX_PROCESSES];
int process_count = 0;

void handleSignals() {
    struct sigaction sa = {{0}};
    sa.sa_handler = SIG_IGN; // Ignore the signal handler
    if (sigaction(SIGCHLD, &sa, NULL) == -1) {
        perror("Failed to set up signal handling");
    }
}

void waitForChild() {
    int status = 0;
    while (waitpid(-1, &status, 0) > 0); // Wait for child process to finish.
}

int manageRequests(/* Add request data here */) {
    handleSignals(); // Ignore SIGCHLD signals

    pid_t pid = fork();
    if (pid == 0) { // Child process
        /* Process HTTP request */
        return 1; // Successful processing of the request.
    } else if (pid < 0) {
        perror("Failed to fork");
        return 0; // Failure to manage requests.
    } else { // Parent process
        pids[process_count++] = pid;
        if (process_count == MAX_PROCESSES) {
            waitForChild();
            process_count--;
        }
    }
    return 1;
}
```
This function `manageRequests()` is used to manage HTTP requests. It first handles SIGCHLD signals, then it forks a new process for each request, and if the maximum number of processes has been reached, it waits for a child process to finish before returning control back to the parent process.

Please note that the use of `fork()` in this context is generally considered bad practice due to its resource-intensive nature. It would be more efficient to use threads instead, or employ a specialized library like libuv or Node.js's cluster forking processes. This example is only meant as a basic demonstration and does not consider all factors that could impact the efficiency of the system at scale.
