For the C# part, you can use ADO.NET to execute the SQL query and return an IDataReader for the result set:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Data;
using System.Data.SqlClient;

public class SqlQueryExecutor
{
    public IDataReader Execute(List<int> values, string connectionString, string commandText)
    {
        if (values == null || values.Count == 0) throw new ArgumentException("Values cannot be empty", nameof(values));

        using (var connection = new SqlConnection(connectionString))
        {
            connection.Open();

            using (var command = new SqlCommand(commandText, connection))
            {
                var parameterNames = new List<string>();

                for (int i = 0; i < values.Count; ++i)
                {
                    string paramName = $"@param{i}";
                    parameterNames.Add(paramName);

                    command.Parameters.AddWithValue(paramName, values[i]);
                }

                commandText += $" WHERE Value IN ({string.Join(", ", parameterNames)})";

                return command.ExecuteReader();
            }
        }
    }
}
```
In this C# example, the `Execute` method accepts a list of integer values to be used as parameters for the SQL query, a database connection string and a SQL command text. It opens a new SqlConnection using the provided connection string, creates a parameterized SQLCommand with the given commandText and each value in the list added as a parameter, then executes the command and returns an IDataReader to read the result set. The method uses `IDisposable` pattern for managing resources, ensuring that they are properly disposed of when no longer needed.
