["Here is the C++ code for the function `insertAfterFirstOccurrence`. This function uses `std::tolower` to convert both strings to lowercase before comparison and handles empty vectors correctly:

```cpp
#include <algorithm>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

void insertAfterFirstOccurrence(std::vector<std::string>& vec, const std::string& target, const std::string& insert) {
    auto it = std::find_if(vec.begin(), vec.end(), [&target](const std::string& str){
        return std::equal(str.begin(), str.end(), target.begin(), 
            [](char a, char b){ return std::tolower(a) == std::tolower(b); });
    });

    if (it != vec.end()) {
        it = vec.insert(++it, insert);

        for (;;) {
            it = std::find_if(++it, vec.end(), [&target](const std::string& str){
                return std::equal(str.begin(), str.end(), target.begin(), 
                    [](char a, char b){ return std::tolower(a) == std::tolower(b); });
            });

            if (it != vec.end()) {
                it = vec.insert(++it, insert);
            } else {
                break;
            }
        }
    } else {
        vec.push_back(insert);
    }
}
```
This function iterates through the vector and uses `std::equal` to compare each element with the target string after converting both strings to lowercase for case-insensitive comparison. If a match is found, it inserts the second argument directly after the first occurrence in the vector. The process then repeats for all subsequent occurrences of the target string.

If no match is found in the entire vector, the function simply pushes back the second argument into the vector. This solution has a time complexity of O(n^2) and space complexity of O(1), where n is the number of elements in the vector. This can be improved by using more efficient algorithms for finding occurrences or for performing case-insensitive comparisons, but it's hard to do better without knowing more about how the function will interact with other parts of the system and what the requirements are.
"]

