["Here's a rough blueprint for such a function. The function `generate_data()` generates an array of struct pointers and assigns each element to point to a heap allocated structure initialized with random values. It also checks if memory allocation fails, if so it sets an error code and returns. 

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <time.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <malloc.h>

#define MAX_STRUCTS 10000
#define ALLOC_THRESHOLD 0.05 /* allocate when free memory is less than 5% */

typedef struct {
    char str[11]; /* room for a 10-character string plus null terminator */
    int num;
    double real;
} MyStruct;

/* error codes: 0 = success, 1 = allocation failure, 2 = memory check failed */
int generate_data(MyStruct ***structs) {
    srand((unsigned int)time(NULL)); /* seed the random number generator */
    *structs = malloc(sizeof(MyStruct*) * MAX_STRUCTS);
    if (*structs == NULL) {
        return 1; /* allocation failure */
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < MAX_STRUCTS; ++i) {
        MyStruct *data = malloc(sizeof(MyStruct));
        if (data == NULL) {
            free(*structs); 
            return 1; /* allocation failure */
        }

        /* populate the struct with random values */
        for (int j = 0; j < 10; ++j) {
            data->str[j] = 'A' + rand() % 26; /* generate a random alphanumeric string */
        }
        data->str[10] = '\0';

        data->num = rand() % 101;
        data->real = (double)rand() / RAND_MAX;

        (*structs)[i] = data;
    }

    /* check if system's available memory is less than a threshold */
    struct mallinfo info = mallinfo();
    unsigned long freeMem = info.fordblks + (info.fsmblks % info.arena);
    if ((freeMem / 100) < (mallinfo().arena * ALLOC_THRESHOLD)) {
        return 2; /* memory check failed */
    }

    return 0; /* success */
}
```
This function follows good practices for C programming, including efficient use of dynamic memory allocation and error handling. It also takes into account the system's available memory to prevent further allocations that could result in out-of-memory errors. However, it does not handle multi-threaded environments or race conditions due to its simplicity and focus on single-threaded operation. For those considerations, a more sophisticated solution would be required that includes mutexes for synchronization, atomic operations for updates, or other concurrency control mechanisms depending on the specifics of your project and environment.
"]

