['Here's a simple implementation of the task using C# and .NET. This code uses an XML file to fetch configuration data and external APIs for fetching additional parameters, while ensuring that all resources are properly freed when the program finishes or encounters errors. However, please note that handling memory allocation failures in low-level languages like C is quite challenging and this implementation does not demonstrate it well.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Linq;
using System.Xml.Linq;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Runtime.InteropServices;

public struct DataPoint
{
    public double Value;
    public string Label;
}

public class Program
{
    private static List<DataPoint> dataPoints = new List<DataPoint>();
    private const long MaxMemoryUsage = 1024 * 1024; // 1 MB

    public static void Main()
    {
        try
        {
            InitializeDataPoints(5, 0.0, 10.0);

            foreach (var dataPoint in dataPoints)
            {
                Console.WriteLine("Value: " + dataPoint.Value + ", Label: " + dataPoint.Label);
            }
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            Console.WriteLine(ex.Message);
            FreeAllAllocatedMemory();
        }
    }

    public static void InitializeDataPoints(int numPoints, double minValue, double maxValue)
    {
        // Load the XML file
        XDocument config = XDocument.Load("config.xml");

        // Parse relevant configuration data
        string labelPrefix = config.Descendants("Label").First().Value;

        // Fetch additional parameters from an external API or other data source
        // ...

        Random random = new Random();
        long memoryUsage = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < numPoints; i++)
        {
            DataPoint dataPoint;

            // Allocate and initialize the double field value
            dataPoint.Value = minValue + random.NextDouble() * (maxValue - minValue);

            // Dynamically allocate a string label using Marshal.StringToHGlobalAnsi for simplicity
            string label = labelPrefix + i;
            IntPtr ptr = Marshal.StringToHGlobalAnsi(label);

            dataPoint.Label = Marshal.PtrToStringAnsi(ptr);

            // Track memory usage
            memoryUsage += (long)Marshal.SizeOf<DataPoint>();

            if (memoryUsage > MaxMemoryUsage)
            {
                FreeAllAllocatedMemory();
                throw new Exception("Exceeded maximum memory limit.");
            }

            dataPoints.Add(dataPoint);
        }
    }

    public static void FreeAllAllocatedMemory()
    {
        // Deallocate all allocated strings before exiting the program
        foreach (var dataPoint in dataPoints)
        {
            IntPtr ptr = Marshal.StringToHGlobalAnsi(dataPoint.Label);
            Marshal.FreeHGlobal(ptr);
        }
    }
}
```
This code demonstrates how to dynamically allocate a string label using `Marshal.StringToHGlobalAnsi` and tracks memory usage by summing the size of each `DataPoint` structure added to the list. When the total memory usage exceeds 1 MB, it throws an exception and frees all allocated strings before exiting.
']

