['The provided task is a complex one that requires understanding of memory management, file I/O operations, concurrent programming and efficient resource usage. Here is an example C solution that implements the requirements:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <pthread.h>

typedef struct data_entry {
    double value;
    char* str;
} data_entry;

pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

int read_data(FILE *file, data_entry **data, int max_size, size_t limit) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock); // Start of critical section

    char buffer[1024];
    int count = 0;
    size_t total_mem = 0;

    while (fgets(buffer, sizeof(buffer), file) && count < max_size) {
        double value;
        char *str = malloc(100); // Assuming fixed length of 100 characters

        if (sscanf(buffer, "%lf %100s", &value, str) != 2) {
            free(str);
            break;
        }

        size_t mem = sizeof(data_entry) + strlen(str) + 1; // Account for struct and string memory usage
        if (total_mem + mem > limit) {
            free(str);
            pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock); // End of critical section
            return count ? 0 : -2; // Return error code if no data entries read successfully
        }

        total_mem += mem;
        data[count] = malloc(sizeof(data_entry)); // Allocate memory for new data entry
        data[count]->value = value;
        data[count]->str = str;
        count++;
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock); // End of critical section
    return count > 0 ? 0 : -1; // Return error code if no data entries read at all
}

void free_data(data_entry **data, int size) {
    for (int i = 0; i < size; ++i) {
        free(data[i]->str);
        free(data[i]);
    }
}
```
This code uses `fgets` to read lines one by one from the file and `sscanf` to parse each line into a double value and a string. The memory for `data_entry` structures is allocated dynamically using `malloc`, ensuring that the total memory usage does not exceed a specified limit.

To ensure thread safety, we use a mutex lock around the critical section of code that modifies shared data (i.e., array of pointers to `data_entry`). The lock is acquired at the beginning and released at the end of the function that reads from the file.

The `free_data` function frees all allocated memory, including the string within each `data_entry` structure. It should be called after use of the data entries to prevent memory leaks. Note that it's important to call this function with an appropriate size argument (i.e., number of successfully read lines) to avoid freeing uninitialized or incorrectly initialized memory.
']

