["Here is a simple example in C of how to implement this functionality. This code doesn't include external data sources fetching, logging capabilities, unit tests and it doesn't handle all error cases that can occur during runtime. 

```C
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <time.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <stdio.h>

// For simplicity, let's assume UEID and CCE are int arrays for this example
typedef struct {
    int* UEID;
    int* CCE;
    bool CRC;
} CCCI;

typedef struct {
    pthread_mutex_t mutex;
    size_t capacity;
    size_t length;
    CCCI** list;
} DynamicList;

void initDynamicList(DynamicList* list, size_t initialCapacity) {
    list->capacity = initialCapacity;
    list->length = 0;
    list->list = (CCCI**)malloc(sizeof(CCCI*) * initialCapacity);
    pthread_mutex_init(&list->mutex, NULL);
}

void addToList(DynamicList* list, CCCI* item) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&list->mutex);

    if (list->length == list->capacity) {
        // Resize the array when full
        list->capacity *= 2;
        list->list = realloc(list->list, sizeof(CCCI*) * list->capacity);
    }

    list->list[list->length++] = item;

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&list->mutex);
}

void updateList(DynamicList* list, CCCI** newItems, size_t count) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&list->mutex);

    if (count > list->capacity - list->length) {
        // Resize the array when not enough free space
        while(list->capacity < list->length + count) 
            list->capacity *= 2;

        list->list = realloc(list->list, sizeof(CCCI*) * list->capacity);
    }

    memcpy(&list->list[list->length], newItems, count * sizeof(CCCI*));
    list->length += count;

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&list->mutex);
}

void freeList(DynamicList* list) {
    for (size_t i = 0; i < list->length; i++) 
        free(list->list[i]);

    pthread_mutex_destroy(&list->mutex);
}
```
This code provides a dynamically resizing thread safe list of CCCI structures. It uses a mutex to ensure that only one thread can modify the list at any given time, minimizing performance overhead. 

The function `addToList` will resize the array when needed and add an item to the end of it. The function `updateList` will also resize the array if there is not enough free space for the new items and then adds all new items to the end of the list. 

This code does not include logging, error checking or fetching external data sources updates as it would require a more complex solution which goes beyond the scope of this request. Also, you might need to adjust the code according to your actual needs, like changing array size handling strategy, thread safety implementation etc. 

For testing, you should create a separate unit tests which covers all edge cases and concurrency scenarios.
"]

