['Here is a general structure of how you can implement this in C using POSIX threads for thread safety:

```c
#include <pthread.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

// Adjust the following depending on your hardware management library
typedef struct DeviceContext {
    char ip_address[16];
    unsigned char mac_address[6];
    int config_flags;
} DeviceContext;

typedef struct Node {
    DeviceContext *data;
    struct Node* next;
} Node;

Node* head = NULL;
pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

void insert(DeviceContext* data) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    Node* newNode = (Node*)malloc(sizeof(Node));
    if (newNode == NULL) exit(1);  // Memory allocation error

    newNode->data = malloc(sizeof(DeviceContext));
    if (newNode->data == NULL) {
        free(newNode);
        exit(2);  // Memory allocation error
    }

    memcpy(newNode->data, data, sizeof(DeviceContext));

    newNode->next = head;
    head = newNode;

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}

void remove() {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    if (head == NULL) return;  // Nothing to remove

    Node* temp = head;
    head = head->next;

    free(temp->data);
    free(temp);

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}
```
This is a simple implementation of a thread-safe linked list. It can be extended to include more complex behavior such as updating device configurations, integrating with network monitoring tools or logging systems, handling external data sources etc. 

One possible approach for integration issues could involve creating an interface that allows the user to provide their own functions for memory allocation and deallocation, event processing, and network-specific operations. This would allow the function to be adapted to different hardware management libraries, monitoring tools, or logging systems without requiring significant modifications. 

For handling external data sources, you could create a separate thread that processes events from the queue. This thread should block when there are no new events in order to avoid latency issues, and it can check whether a network change has been processed before processing the next event. 

In terms of performance constraints, careful attention needs to be given to memory usage and CPU cycles. The linked list is simple and efficient for small amounts of data but may not perform well with large volumes of data or high concurrency. Solutions such as using a balanced binary search tree or a hash table can help optimize read and write operations.
