['Here is a sample implementation in C that creates an array of NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T structures, populates it with random RX_UE_INFORMATION_TAG, RNTI, and randomly set CRCFLAG. It also handles memory allocation/deallocation for each structure to avoid leaks, and checks if N is a positive integer and does not exceed 1000:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <time.h>
#include <pthread.h> // For thread safety

typedef struct {
    int UE_INFORMATION;
    int RNTI;
} RX_UE_INFORMATION_TAG;

typedef struct {
    int CRCFLAG;
} CRC_INDICATION_REL8_TAG;

typedef struct {
    RX_UE_INFORMATION_TAG infoTag;
    CRC_INDICATION_REL8_TAG rel8Tag;
} NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T;

pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER; // For thread safety

NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T* createStructures(int N) {
    if (N <= 0 || N > 1000) {
        printf("Invalid input: N must be a positive integer and not exceed 1000\n");
        return NULL;
    }

    // Thread safe allocation
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);
    NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T* structures = (NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T*) malloc(N * sizeof(NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T));
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);

    if (structures == NULL) {
        printf("Memory allocation failed\n");
        return NULL;
    }

    srand(time(NULL)); // seed the random number generator with current time

    for (int i = 0; i < N; ++i) {
        structures[i].infoTag.UE_INFORMATION = rand(); // Generate a random UE information tag
        structures[i].infoTag.RNTI = rand(); // Generate a random RNTI

        structures[i].rel8Tag.CRCFLAG = rand() % 2; // Randomly set CRCFLAG to 0 or 1
    }

    return structures;
}

void destroyStructures(NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T* structures) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);
    free(structures); // Thread safe deallocation
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}
```
This code uses `pthread_mutex_t` for thread safety. When a function needs to modify the shared data, it locks the mutex beforehand and unlocks it afterward to ensure only one thread can access the data at any given time. This helps prevent race conditions and improves performance by avoiding unnecessary context switches when multiple threads are trying to access the same resources simultaneously.

The memory allocation and deallocation for each structure is also handled carefully. In case of an error during memory allocation, we make sure to unlock the mutex before returning.
